*.tgz
.DS_Store
# *.log: NOT LaTeX-scoped (see master-thesis-latex/.gitignore for those --
# LaTeX's own .log needs the exact-name form "*.log", not a subpath, since
# it's written next to each .tex file). Deliberately still repo-wide too:
# confirmed live it also (correctly) excludes runtime-generated PHP error
# logs (wp-content/uploads/wc-logs/fatal-errors-*.log) -- same class of
# "regenerated content, not source" as wflogs/ below.
*.log
*.sql.gz
*.tar.gz
# ...except backup_service's own backups, which ids/backups/
# .gitattributes already routes through git-lfs -- these two blanket
# rules above were silently shadowing that: `git check-ignore` confirmed
# every real backup file was being ignored regardless, so despite the
# LFS plumbing existing, zero backup files had ever actually reached git.
# This does NOT mean backups are committed automatically -- it means a
# backup you've deliberately chosen to keep (labeled via the dashboard's
# Backups page or scripts/manage_backups.sh, then `git add`ed) CAN be,
# rather than being invisibly dropped by `git add` regardless of intent.
!ids/backups/db/*.sql.gz
!ids/backups/wp/*.tar.gz

# Wordfence's own runtime security-log directory (attack-data.php,
# config*.php, ips.php, rules.php, ...) -- regenerated by the plugin on
# every request/scan, not source content. Confirmed live: shows up as
# modified/new files in git status continuously even with zero real
# changes to the site.
ids/production_eshop_files/wp-content/wflogs/

# Auto-generated by WordPress's docker-entrypoint.sh from the
# WORDPRESS_DB_* env vars (DB credentials, unique auth salts) on first
# boot -- runtime config, not source, and not meant to be shared between
# environments (each deployment should get its own salts). Was
# previously untracked-but-not-ignored: harmless as long as nobody ran
# `git add -A`/`git add .` in production_eshop_files/, but that's exactly
# the kind of mistake this file is meant to prevent.
ids/production_eshop_files/wp-config.php
